==Transportation==
{{main|Transportation in Okami|Okami Metro}}
Okami is the central logistical hub of Okaiken's domestic and international transit networks. The city is served by the Okami Metro, an expansive rapid transit network comprising 12 lines and over 140 stations, which moves millions of commuters daily. Following the privatization of the system in 2018, the network has become heavily reliant on the Satori algorithm to optimize train frequencies and manage crowds during peak industrial shift changes.

For national travel, Okami Central Station serves as the primary terminus for the high-speed Harujima Shinkansen and the broader Okaiken Railways (OR Harujima) commuter network, connecting the capital directly to major cities like Toyozawa and Shinagawa. International and domestic aviation is handled primarily by Okami-Tobu International Airport, which serves as the flagship hub for the national carrier, Okaiken Air, maintaining critical trans-Althenian corridors for the Sanese economy.

